 ## Current state
 
-The Next.js app and the Supabase schema are scaffolded (auth, orgs, list infrastructure, core CRM writes, since Phase 3 events with their lineup, since Phase 4 operations: tasks, meetings, notes and documents behind a private-bucket, malware-scanned file pipeline, since Phase 5 event-level finance, since Phase 6 **the network**: publishable profiles for companies/artists/venues/agencies, a browsable directory, request-to-connect, and an explicit, manual private-record-to-network-identity link, and — since Phase 7 — the operator's own surfaces: a searchable cross-tenant **audit log viewer** at `/super/audit-log` **break-glass impersonation**, the product's only sanctioned deep-access path, and **organisation lifecycle** at `/super/organisations` — suspend, close and reopen a workspace, soft-delete only, every transition reasoned and audited and every one of them reversible, and — since Phase 7 Task 4 — **billing and feature overrides** at `/super/billing`: a time-boxed, audited exception to one workspace's effective tier, seat count or a named feature flag, routed through a single shared effective-tier function on each side (`private.effective_organisation_plan()` in SQL, `getOrganisationEntitlements()` in TypeScript) so every existing tier gate (CRM writes, custom-field limits, the finance module, network publishing) picks up an active override automatically, with no scheduler needed to revert it — an override simply stops being read once it expires), and — since Phase 7 Task 5 — **platform health at `/super/health`** with the **rate limiting and incident alerting** behind it: `SECURITY.md` § 5's whole table is now built as one mechanism whose values live in Postgres, metered at the single `lib/list/query.ts` list chokepoint for search and the named network-directory path and in GoTrue's own password-verification hook for the per-account login lockout, with the counters it writes doubling as what the three incident triggers read, and — since Phase 7 Task 6 — **in-app feedback**: a "Feedback" dialog on every signed-in screen writing a platform-scoped `feedback` table whose attribution and 10/hour budget are both enforced at the `INSERT`, a founder's list at `/super/feedback` with audited screenshot views, and the **one Brevo sender** (`lib/email/send.ts`) that emails the feedback receipt and delivers the incident-alert digest, and — since Phase 7 Task 6b — **every INSERT and UPDATE on the eleven mutable Phase 2 CRM tables audited by trigger**, with a name-only diff (changed column names, values for a per-table structural allowlist only — never tenant content, because the audit log is superuser-readable), soft-delete/restore as their own actions, and one correlation id per multi-row RPC (`SECURITY.md` § 7) — **and, since Phase 8 Task 7 (Q122), on sixteen more: every Phase 3–6 table a client can write plus `organisation_memberships`, `organisation_subscriptions` and `merge_candidates`, derived by `private.audit_coverage()` from the live catalogue so a later table with a client grant and no audit writer is a red census test naming itself; `organisations` itself (an Owner's rename) is the one declared excusal, Q189** — and — since Phase 8 Task 1 — **the fifteen PRD §12 legal pages**: `/legal` and `/legal/<slug>`, readable signed out, each rendering the current published version of a document from a versioned store (`public.legal_documents`) that the founder edits at `/super/legal` without a deploy — drafts, a preview, step-up-gated publishing and a per-version counsel-review marker, with **every published version immutable for every role** and **every page carrying a non-dismissable "draft pending legal review" notice until counsel's review of that exact version is recorded** (none has been: all fifteen are engineering drafts; see § Legal and regulatory compliance), and — since Phase 8 Task 2 — **a consent ledger and an account-level marketing preference**: `public.consents`, one append-only row per grant or withdrawal, attributed from the session and pointing at the exact published legal version, edited at `/account/privacy` with the same single button either way; and, instead of a cookie banner (Q129), **a storage census** — a source scan in CI of every app-shipped file for the cookie, storage, script and tracker shapes `SECURITY.md` § 8 lists, plus a browser check that runs only on a developer machine — that fails when an undeclared one appears in a shape it recognises (see § Legal and regulatory compliance), and — since Phase 8 Task 3 — **data subject requests**: `/account/data-requests`, where any signed-in person exercises the six UK/EU GDPR rights and tracks what happened, and `/super/data-requests`, the operator's queue ordered by deadline, with the **30 days computed and enforced by the database** (a `GENERATED ALWAYS` `due_by` no role can write, a state machine in a `BEFORE UPDATE` trigger rather than labels on a column, operator notes in a separate table the requester has no read of at all, and an overdue or approaching deadline arriving as an `incident_alerts` row on `/super/health` and in the existing email digest, written by the existing `pg_cron` job rather than a second one). and, since Phase 8 Task 4 — **the two data exports Q132's controller/processor split makes necessary**: `/account/exports`, where anyone signed in takes a machine-readable copy of what the PLATFORM holds about them as a user, and `/[orgSlug]/settings/export`, where an Owner or Admin takes one of everything the WORKSPACE holds — deleted rows included and marked as deleted, with the files themselves — both built outside the request that asked for them, both bounded by ceilings a job fails against rather than truncates, both delivered as a zip in a private bucket behind a server-mediated, audited, expiring download that only the requester can use, and the workspace one **built with the requester's own access rights** so a colleague's private notes are not in it. Marking an account-scope access or portability request `fulfilled` now REQUIRES such an export to have run for that person since they asked, and — since Phase 8 Task 5 — **the other half of that: account erasure**. `/account/erase` deletes a person's account for good, behind step-up and a typed confirmation phrase, with the page saying specifically what goes and what stays before anything is pressed; `/super/data-requests` gains the control that runs one for a statutory request, and marking an account-scope `erasure` request `fulfilled` now requires the job to have actually run. **The audit trail is pseudonymised, never deleted (Q130):** thirteen foreign keys reference `auth.users` with no `ON DELETE` action, and every one is rewritten — by a walk of `pg_constraint` at run time, not by a list — to a single platform-owned tombstone identity that can never sign in, never be a member of anything and never be deleted. The whole sequence is ONE database function in ONE transaction: all-or-nothing (any refusal rolls it back and records a reason; there is no partial erasure), idempotent, refusing to run under `session_replication_role = replica` or while a byte of the account's stored files is still in its bucket. A private note is HARD-deleted because nulling its owner leaves it readable by nobody and stored for ever; a workspace's own records stay, because Q132 makes the business their controller. The last Owner of a workspace with people or records in it is REFUSED, in the database, with a sentence naming the workspace (Q131). `private.erase_organisation()` ships beside it as the primitive Task 6's 60-day purge calls, and — since Phase 8 Task 6 — **the retention and garbage-collection job that calls it**. One `pg_cron`-scheduled sweep, which is the FIFTH half of the product's one and only tick rather than a second clock: it purges the bytes of an expired soft-deleted document (Q52), an expired feedback row and its screenshot (Q120), stale rate-limit counters (Q110), resolved incident alerts and their subject-id JSON (Q117), a failed erasure job at 90 days (Q175 — a succeeded one is kept for ever, because it is pseudonymous and it is the proof), a job left stuck `running` (Q166 — FAILED, not deleted; what is released is the index that was locking somebody out of a statutory right), an export archive past its own `expires_at`, orphaned objects in all three buckets, a workspace 60 days after it was closed (Q88/Q92/Q102, by calling the Task 5 primitive), and — behind five separable rails — the AUDIT LOG at one year. **Every period lives in ONE declared function** (`private.retention_periods()`), so counsel's answer to Q133 is one migration; **every table in `public` is classified** by a roll whose derivation refuses the job outright when a later phase adds one nobody has decided about. **The audit purge is the dangerous one and is built as `SECURITY.md` § 7's "narrowly controlled platform security process":** a hard floor of one year that no configuration can lower, its own kill switch, a sanity ceiling that refuses a run deleting more than a fifth of the table, a bounded resumable per-run ceiling, and the totals before and after in the same transaction. The founder can stop the job (or just its audit purge) without a deploy, can PREVIEW what a run would delete, and — since Phase 8 Task 9a (Q184, founder 2026-09-30) — can RELEASE the sanity ceiling for exactly 3 days, behind step-up and a reason, lapsing by read-time expiry with the floor and the per-run ceiling still in force, from `/super/health`, where the last five runs are rendered with their counts and — the column that matters — what each one refused. And Q179: an unsanctioned `delete from auth.users` (the Supabase Studio Auth-page button) now REFUSES rather than stranding a live workspace with no owner. **That job's review graded it MAJOR and its fix round 1 changed four things a reader should not have to find:** (C1) **the document retention clock was writable by the tenant** — `authenticated` held table-wide INSERT/UPD